1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,578
Excel's Remove Duplicates button is useful, but the wrong key can delete real records.

2
00:00:05,578 --> 00:00:07,306
Look at these two rows.

3
00:00:07,306 --> 00:00:12,836
Both customers are named Alex Lee, but they have different email addresses and different customer IDs.

4
00:00:12,836 --> 00:00:18,174
If you remove duplicates using only the Name column, one real customer can disappear.

5
00:00:18,174 --> 00:00:20,765
Instead, decide what should actually be unique.

6
00:00:20,765 --> 00:00:25,576
For customer data, that might be a customer ID or another verified key.

7
00:00:25,576 --> 00:00:30,938
Before deleting anything, flag the duplicates first and review the affected rows.

8
00:00:30,938 --> 00:00:36,740
The rule is simple: deduplicate by a trusted key, not by a convenient-looking column.

9
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:40,884
That keeps cleanup fast without silently throwing away valid data.
